{"id":"GRAM-FOCUS-001","category":"focus_particle_subject_clitic","status":"provisional","particle_family":["baa","ayaa"],"structure":"subject_before_focused_object_before_verb","subject_person":"third","subject_position":"before_focused_non_subject_np","clitic_requirement":"required","positive_examples":["Wiilku muus buu cunay.","Maryan muus bay cuntay."],"negative_examples":["Wiilku muus baa cunay.","Maryan muus baa cuntay."],"note":"Native-speaker review agrees that a third-person subject before the focused object requires the matching subject clitic: masculine buu, feminine bay. SLS gives the same required-clitic pattern.","source":"SLS resources/naxwe/09-weer-fudud.md","review_evidence":"native_speaker_project_review"}
{"id":"GRAM-FOCUS-002","category":"focus_particle_subject_clitic","status":"context_required","particle_family":["baa","ayaa"],"structure":"focused_object_before_subject_before_verb","subject_person":"third","subject_position":"after_focused_non_subject_np_before_verb","clitic_requirement":"disputed","source_examples":["Moos buu wiilkii cunay.","Moos baa wiilkii cunay."],"reviewed_preferred_examples":["Muus ayuu wiilku cunay.","Wiilku muus buu cunay."],"native_review_interpretation":{"surface":"Moos baa wiilkii cunay.","reviewer_reading":"The banana ate the boy.","intended_boy_eats_banana_reading":false,"evidence":"native_speaker_project_review"},"note":"SLS lists bare baa as optional in this ordering, but native-speaker project review reads 'Moos baa wiilkii cunay' with reversed semantic roles rather than as 'the boy ate the banana.' Do not teach, validate, or auto-correct this structure until broader evidence resolves the conflict.","source":"SLS resources/naxwe/09-weer-fudud.md","review_evidence":"native_speaker_project_review"}
{"id":"GRAM-FOCUS-003","category":"focus_particle_subject_clitic","status":"provisional","particle_family":["baa","ayaa"],"structure":"focused_object_before_verb_before_subject","subject_person":"third","subject_position":"after_verb","clitic_requirement":"required","positive_example":"Moos buu cunay wiilkii.","negative_example":"Moos baa cunay wiilkii.","note":"SLS describes a required subject clitic when the subject follows the verb. This pattern has not yet received project native-speaker validation and remains reference-only.","source":"SLS resources/naxwe/09-weer-fudud.md"}
{"id":"GRAM-FOCUS-004","category":"focus_particle_subject_clitic","status":"provisional","particle_family":["baa","ayaa"],"structure":"first_or_second_person_subject_with_focused_non_subject_np","subject_person":["first","second"],"subject_position":"any","clitic_requirement":"required","positive_example":"Adigu moos baad cuntay.","negative_example":"Adigu moos baa cuntay.","note":"For first- or second-person subjects, the subject clitic attached to baa/ayaa is required regardless of subject position in the structures described by the source.","source":"SLS resources/naxwe/09-weer-fudud.md"}
{"id":"GRAM-FOCUS-005","category":"focus_particle_subject_clitic","status":"provisional","particle_family":["baa","ayaa"],"structure":"third_person_feminine_subject_before_focused_non_subject_np","subject_person":"third","subject_gender":"feminine","subject_position":"before_focused_non_subject_np","clitic_requirement":"required","positive_example":"Maryan muus bay cuntay.","negative_example":"Maryan muus baa cuntay.","note":"Native-speaker project review confirms the feminine singular pattern Maryan + focused object + bay + feminine verb form. SLS gives the same required-clitic contrast.","source":"SLS resources/naxwe/09-weer-fudud.md","review_evidence":"native_speaker_project_review"}
